[{"record_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_002410","candidate_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_002410","source_record_id":"rec_002410","source_corpus":"include","title":"seqLens: optimizing language models for genomic predictions","abstract":"Understanding evolutionary variation in genomic sequences through the lens of language modeling has the potential to revolutionize biological research. Yet to maximize the utility of language modeling in genomics, we must overcome computational challenges in tokenization and model architecture adapted to diverse genomic features across evolutionary timescales. In this study, we investigated key elements in genomic language modeling (gLM), including tokenization, pretraining datasets, fine-tuning approaches, pooling methods, and domain adaptation, and applied the language models to diverse genomic data. We gathered two evolutionarily distinct pretraining datasets: one consisting of 19,551 reference genomes, including over 18,000 prokaryotic genomes (115B nucleotides) and the remainder eukaryotic genomes, and another more balanced dataset with 1,354 genomes, including 1,166 prokaryotic and 188 eukaryotic reference genomes (180B nucleotides). We trained five byte-pair encoding tokenizers and pretrained 52 gLMs, systematically comparing different architectures, hyperparameters, and classification heads. We introduce seqLens , a family of models based on disentangled attention with relative positional encoding, which outperforms relatively similar-sized models in 13 of 19 benchmarking phenotypic predictions. We further explore continual pretraining, domain adaptation, and parameter-efficient fine-tuning methods to assess trade-offs between computational efficiency and accuracy. [truncated]","doi":"10.1101/2025.03.12.642848","year":"","venue":"","sources":[],"full_text_context":"[abstract: Abstract]\nUnderstanding evolutionary variation in genomic sequences through the lens of language modeling has the potential to revolutionize biological research. Yet to maximize the utility of language modeling in genomics, we must overcome computational challenges in tokenization and model architecture adapted to diverse genomic features across evolutionary timescales. In this study, we investigated key elements in genomic language modeling (gLM), including tokenization, pretraining datasets, fine-tuning approaches, pooling methods, and domain adaptation, and applied the language models to diverse genomic data. We gathered two evolutionarily distinct pretraining datasets: one consisting of 19,551 reference genomes, including over 18,000 prokaryotic genomes (115B nucleotides) and the remainder eukaryotic genomes, and another more balanced dataset with 1,354 genomes, including 1,166 prokaryotic and 188 eukaryotic reference genomes (180B nucleotides). We trained five byte-pair encoding tokenizers and pretrained 52 gLMs, systematically comparing different architectures, hyperparameters, and classification heads. We introduce seqLens , a family of models based on disentangled attention with relative positional encoding, which outperforms relatively similar-sized models in 13 of 19 benchmarking phenotypic predictions. We further explore continual pretraining, domain adaptation, and parameter-efficient fine-tuning methods to assess trade-offs between computational efficiency and accuracy. [truncated]\n\n[discussion_conclusion: Discussion]\nDNA sequences can be conceptualized as a complex language, rich with unseen pat354 terns that hold the key to understanding fundamental biological insights [1-3]. Recent 355 advancements in language modeling technology have revolutionized the ability to 356 decipher these hidden patterns, offering faster, more efficient, and versatile methods 357 compared to traditional alignment-based approaches [2, 3, 24]. Unlike conventional 358 techniques that focus primarily on individual regions of DNA, language models pro359 vide a novel framework for exploring intricate interactions among regions, uncovering 360 a deeper layer of genomic complexity. This paradigm shift aligns with the sequential 361 nature of many biological techniques, such as mass spectrometry for protein analysis, 362\n\n363\n\n364\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nUMAP 1\n\nModels\n\nFig. 9 : Effect of Domain-Adaptive Pretraining (DAP-training) on Model Performance Across Tasks. a : The base-model underwent an additional 150K training steps with three datasets: prokaryote-only genome ( Mp ), eukaryote-only genome ( Me ), and the original general-purpose training dataset ( Ms ). The resulting models were evaluated on benchmarking datasets. Boxplots are based on the validation scores (circles), and the triangle represents the models' performance on the test set. b : The figure compares the 2d representation of token embeddings of models. Three sample tokens were marked to highlight the changes in representations. c : The distribution of token embedding distances from the base-model . [truncated]\n\n[data_representation: and ESM [27, 28] architectures and introduce seqLens models. We systematically 75 investigate dynamic tokenization using byte-pair encoding (BPE), diverse pretrain76 ing datasets spanning multiple species, architectural features such as disentangled 77 attention, parameter-efficient fine-tuning approaches, different pooling methods, and 78 continual pretraining for domain adaptation (Figure 1). We also evaluate our models' 79 understanding of evolutionary relationships by analyzing the embeddings and com80 paring performance against existing genomic language models. Our comprehensive 81 benchmarking demonstrates that these methodological advances significantly enhance 82 genomic language model performance, with one of the seqLens models outperforming 83 similar-sized models in 13 of 19 benchmarking tasks, establishing a robust framework 84 for developing future genomic language models that are more effective, adaptable, and 85 capable of addressing complex biological questions. 20 Base -Mean Base -Max Base -Mean Base -Max]\n20\n\n10\n\n0\n\n-\n\n10\n\n-\n\n30\n\n20\n\n30\n\nPC2 (13.7%)\n\n20\n\n10\n\n0\n\n-\n\n10\n\n-\n\n20\n\n20\n\n20\n\n10\n\n0\n\n-\n\n-\n\n-\n\n-\n\n20\n\n20\n\n-\n\n10\n\n0\n\n20\n\nPC1 (12.5%)\n\n10\n\n10\n\n20\n\n-\n\n0\n\nPC1 (8.7%)\n\nFine\n\n-\n\ntuned\n\n-\n\nMean\n\n20\n\n-\n\n10\n\n0\n\n10\n\n20\n\n[data_representation: Training dataset]\nFor pretraining and validation, we curated a dataset comprising all 19,551 representative genomes sourced from the National Center for Biotechnology Information (NCBI) database by February 23, 2024, including 18,268 bacterial, 647 archaeal, 577 fungal, 40 viral, and 1 human reference genome. Then, to create samples, we created chunks of 3200 long nucleotide sequences with 100 bases overlapping. The resulting train set has 27,831,882 samples, the validation set has 3,478,985, and the test set has 3,478,986 samples.\n\nFor the second dataset, which is a more diverse dataset in terms of the tree of life, 537 we extended the multi-species pretraining dataset that was collected in the Nucleotide 538\n\nTransformers [24] study and extended it with 499 archaea genomes and 5 eukaryotic 539 genomes (see section 4). 540\n\n541\n\n542\n\n543\n\n544\n\n545\n\n546\n\n547\n\n548\n\n549\n\n550\n\n551\n\n552\n\n553\n\n554\n\n555\n\n556\n\n557\n\n558\n\n559\n\n560\n\n561\n\n562\n\n563\n\n564\n\n565\n\n566\n\n567\n\n568\n\n569\n\n570\n\n571\n\n572\n\n573\n\n574\n\n575\n\n576\n\n577\n\n578\n\n579\n\n580\n\n[data_representation: Benchmarking datasets 732]\n#### 8.6.1 Genome benchmark dataset 733\n\nThe nucleotide transformer downstream tasks dataset represents a comprehensive 734 genomics benchmark comprising 18 downstream tasks, exclusively derived from human 735 samples [57]. The dataset features a mix of binary and multi-class classification tasks, 736 carefully curated from high-quality sources. It offers diverse genomic element classi737 fications across various scales, ranging from 300bp to 1kb sequence lengths, covering 738 histone marks, enhancer elements, promoter regions, and splice sites. We are using 739 the revised version of the dataset that addresses previous methodological limitations 740 by replacing randomly generated negative samples with existing genomic sequences 741 and implementing proper chromosome held-out test sets. The tasks include classifi742 cations of histone modifications, enhancer types (tissue-specific and tissue-invariant), 743 promoter elements (with and without TATA-box motifs), and splice site annotations, 744 providing a robust and consistent framework for genomic machine learning research. 745\n\n746\n\n747\n\n748\n\n749\n\n750\n\n751\n\n752\n\n753\n\n754\n\n755\n\n756\n\n757\n\n758\n\n759\n\n760\n\n761\n\n762\n\n763\n\n764\n\n765\n\n766\n\n767\n\n768\n\n769\n\n770\n\n771\n\n772\n\n#### 8.6.2 Genome origin datasets\n\nTo extend the benchmarking beyond the human genome, we prepared two additional datasets: one for plasmid/chromosome detection and another for the classification of short reads into four categories: prokaryote, eukaryote, archaea and viruses. [truncated]\n\n[data_representation: Genome benchmark dataset 733]\nThe nucleotide transformer downstream tasks dataset represents a comprehensive 734 genomics benchmark comprising 18 downstream tasks, exclusively derived from human 735 samples [57]. The dataset features a mix of binary and multi-class classification tasks, 736 carefully curated from high-quality sources. It offers diverse genomic element classi737 fications across various scales, ranging from 300bp to 1kb sequence lengths, covering 738 histone marks, enhancer elements, promoter regions, and splice sites. We are using 739 the revised version of the dataset that addresses previous methodological limitations 740 by replacing randomly generated negative samples with existing genomic sequences 741 and implementing proper chromosome held-out test sets. The tasks include classifi742 cations of histone modifications, en